{
  "teamId": 158,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Milwaukee Brewers opened the year looking like genuine contenders, racing out to a 15-7 April record that was the best in the National League. The offense was finding timely heroes — Kolten Wong's clutch triple, Andrew McCutchen's veteran savvy — while the pitching staff posted a 3.83 ERA that ranked among baseball's elite. Everything seemed to be clicking in that first month, even as a few bullpen blunders reminded fans that this roster had real vulnerabilities.\n\nMay and the early summer maintained the Brewers' winning ways, powered by unexpected contributions from Keston Hiura, who had battled for his career, and the steady brilliance of the pitching staff. A 17-12 May kept Milwaukee near the top of the NL Central standings. But cracks were emerging. Josh Hader, the cornerstone of the bullpen and a perennial All-Star closer, began showing alarming inconsistency in June and July — surrendering grand slams and key late-inning hits at the worst moments — even as the team posted a winning July record. The Hader situation became a storyline unto itself, with the lefty blowing multiple high-leverage saves and ultimately getting dealt at the trade deadline.\n\nAugust brought chaos. The Brewers stumbled to a 12-15 record as the offense went cold and the bullpen, still adjusting post-Hader, proved unreliable. Victor Caratini and Rowdy Tellez provided power, and rookie Garrett Mitchell offered a spark with his first career home run, but the team couldn't string wins together consistently. The division race tightened, and Milwaukee found itself fighting from behind rather than protecting a lead.\n\nSeptember and early October became a desperate sprint to the finish. The Brewers went 15-13 in September, staying alive mathematically but never quite seizing control. Crushing late-season losses — Freddy Peralta surrendering a walk-off grand slam to Miami, Francisco Lindor's grand slam off Taylor Rogers — kept draining the life from the playoff push. In the final series of the year, the Brewers rallied heroically in back-to-back games against Arizona, with Willy Adames and Kolten Wong delivering clutch hits. But it wasn't enough. A Trevor Gott-allowed home run on October 5th sealed their fate, and the 86-76 Brewers watched the postseason from home — a talented, pitching-rich team that simply ran out of road.",
